A class is a blueprint: it declares what *kind* of state every object built from it will carry (its fields) and what behavior every such object will support (its methods). To have any state to work with you create objects from it with `new`.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private String owner;
    private double balance;

    public BankAccount(String owner, double balance) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
```

`BankAccount` describes the *shape* every account will have (an owner and a balance) but declaring the class compiles no accounts into existence. `new BankAccount("Amy", 100.0)` is what allocates a fresh object on the heap, runs the constructor against it, and hands back a reference to that specific object. Create two accounts and you get two separate objects, each with its own independent copy of `owner` and `balance`. Mutating one's balance has zero effect on the other's, because each *instance* carries its own copy of the instance fields. A `static` field, by contrast, belongs to the class and is shared by every instance, with one copy however many objects exist. Make a field `static` only when the data belongs to the class as a whole, such as a counter used to hand out account numbers.

A constructor looks like a method with no return type, sharing the class's exact name, and it runs automatically, exactly once, every time `new` creates an object. Its job is to get the new object into a valid starting state before anyone else can touch it. If you write no constructor at all, Java supplies a no-argument default constructor that does nothing beyond giving fields their default values (`0`, `false`, `null`). But the moment you write *any* constructor yourself, that free default disappears; if you still want a no-argument way to create the object, you have to write one explicitly.

Classes commonly need more than one way to be constructed, and Java lets you overload constructors exactly like methods: same name (mandatory, since it's always the class name), different parameter lists:

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private String owner;
    private double balance;

    public BankAccount(String owner, double balance) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public BankAccount(String owner) {
        this(owner, 0.0);   // constructor chaining -- delegates to the constructor above
    }
}
```

`this(owner, 0.0)` is **constructor chaining**: it calls another constructor of the *same* class, and in Java 21 it must be the first statement in the constructor body. (Java 25 relaxed this: statements that don't touch the object under construction, such as argument checks, may come before it.) Without chaining, adding a validation rule (say, rejecting a negative opening balance) later would mean hunting down and fixing every constructor that assigns `balance` separately; with chaining, only the one constructor that does the assignment needs to change.

Notice `this.owner = owner;` in the first constructor: the parameter is intentionally named the same as the field it initializes, which is idiomatic and reads clearly at the call site, but it creates a naming collision inside the constructor body. `this.owner` means 'the field belonging to the object being constructed'; a bare `owner` on its own refers to the *parameter*, which shadows the field for the remainder of the constructor. Writing `owner = owner;` by mistake (forgetting `this.`) compiles without error, it's legal Java, and does nothing useful at all: it assigns the parameter to itself, and the field keeps its default value (`null`, for a `String`). Declaring the field `final` turns the mistake into a compile error, because the compiler sees that the field was never assigned.

Making a field `private` is necessary for encapsulation, but it is not, by itself, sufficient. A common mistake is a getter that returns a direct reference to a mutable field, which hands the caller a way to mutate the object's internal state anyway, completely bypassing every rule the class thought it was enforcing.

```java
public class Team {
    private List<String> members;

    public Team() {
        this.members = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public List<String> getMembers() {
        return members;   // leaks the actual internal list!
    }
}

Team team = new Team();
team.getMembers().add("Zoe");   // mutates Team's PRIVATE internal state from outside!
```

`members` is `private`, and there's no public setter for it, it looks encapsulated. But `getMembers()` returns the exact same `List` object that lives inside `team`, not a copy, and lists are mutable. So any caller holding that returned reference can call `.add()`, `.remove()`, or `.clear()` on it and alter `team`'s internal state, with the `Team` class having no idea it happened and no chance to validate or reject it. It's array aliasing again: the getter copies the reference, so caller and object share one list.

The fix is a **defensive copy**: return a new list containing the same elements, so the caller can look at the data (or even mutate their own copy freely) without any of that reaching back into `team`'s internals: `return new ArrayList<>(members);`. `List.copyOf(members)` goes one step further and returns an unmodifiable copy, so a caller who tries to add to it gets an `UnsupportedOperationException` right away. Both copies are shallow: if the elements are themselves mutable, callers can still change them. Constructors leak in the other direction, so copy a list passed *in* before storing it.

The same trap applies to arrays and to any other mutable object type returned from a getter. Primitives and immutable types (like `String`, or numeric wrapper types) don't have this problem at all, because there's no way to 'mutate in place' a value that's immutable.

