`class Dog extends Animal` declares `Dog` a **subclass** of `Animal` (equivalently, `Animal` is `Dog`'s **superclass**). And it means every `Dog` object automatically has, in addition to whatever `Dog` itself declares, the accessible (non-private) fields and methods `Animal` declares too, without `Dog` having to re-declare or re-implement any of them.

```java
public class Animal {
    protected String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating.");
    }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " says woof!");
    }
}

Dog rex = new Dog("Rex");
rex.eat();    // inherited from Animal -- 'Rex is eating.'
rex.bark();   // declared directly on Dog -- 'Rex says woof!'
```

`Dog` never writes an `eat()` method of its own, and yet `rex.eat()` works. It's calling the exact `eat()` method body defined in `Animal`, inherited unchanged. Note the field is declared `protected`, not `private`: `protected` means accessible within this class, within any subclass, and (easy to forget) from any other class in the same package. That lets `Dog`'s methods use `name`. Had `name` been `private` in `Animal`, every `Dog` object would still contain storage for it, but `Dog`'s own code could not refer to it and would have to go through an accessor `Animal` provides. In practice, a private field plus an accessor is often the better choice, because a `protected` field lets every subclass bypass the superclass's checks.

`extends` does *not* hand down constructors: `Dog` does not automatically get `Animal`'s constructor the way it gets `Animal`'s methods. Each subclass has its own constructors, and **every one of them starts by calling a superclass constructor** (or another constructor of the same class, via `this(...)`, which does). If you don't write the call, Java inserts a call to the superclass's no-argument constructor, `super()`, as the first line. If the superclass has no accessible no-argument constructor (`Animal` above only defines one that takes a `name`), the inserted call doesn't compile, and you must write a `super(...)` call that matches a constructor the superclass has.

```java
public class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    public Dog(String name, String breed) {
        super(name);       // must come first (Java 21) -- calls Animal's constructor
        this.breed = breed;
    }
}
```

The reason: before `Dog`'s constructor body uses inherited state like `name`, that state must be initialized, and the code that knows how to do it is `Animal`'s constructor. This cascades all the way up an inheritance chain: constructing a `Dog` first fully runs `Object`'s (implicit) constructor, then `Animal`'s, then finally `Dog`'s own body, top of the hierarchy down, even though you only ever wrote `new Dog(...)`.

A subclass can **override** an inherited method by redeclaring it with the exact same signature (name, parameter types, and a compatible return type) and a new body. This replaces the inherited behavior for objects of that subclass, and it's the mechanism behind what's often called **run-time polymorphism**: which version of the method runs is decided by the object's ** type, not by the declared type of the reference variable pointing at it.

```java
public class Animal {
    public String makeSound() {
        return "...";
    }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public String makeSound() {
        return "Woof!";
    }
}

Animal a = new Dog();       // declared type Animal, ACTUAL type Dog
System.out.println(a.makeSound());   // 'Woof!' -- not '...'
```

`a` is declared as `Animal`, but the object it refers to is a `Dog`, and `makeSound()` dispatches based on that, run-time type, printing `"Woof!"`. Overloading works the other way round:overloading is resolved at *compile time* from the declared types at the call site, overriding at *run time* from the object's type. Only instance methods work this way: `static` methods, `private` methods, and fields are not dispatched at run time, and a `final` method can't be overridden.

The `@Override` annotation isn't required, but always write it. If you misspell the method name or get a parameter type wrong, `@Override` turns what would otherwise be a new, unrelated method into a compile error.

Inside an overriding method, `super.methodName(...)` calls the *superclass's* version of that method, rather than the overriding version. Useful when a subclass wants to *extend* the inherited behavior rather than fully *replace* it.

```java
public class Animal {
    protected String name;

    public String describe() {
        return "An animal named " + name;
    }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return super.describe() + ", a " + breed;   // builds on Animal's version, doesn't replace it
    }
}
```

Without `super.describe()`, `Dog`'s override would have to fully re-derive `"An animal named " + name` itself, duplicating logic that already exists correctly in `Animal`. And if `Animal`'s version of that logic ever changes, `Dog`'s duplicated copy would drift out of sync. `super.describe()` calls exactly one specific version (the superclass's) bypassing the normal run-time dispatch for that one call; it does not, itself, trigger any further overriding lookup. Calling `this.describe()` inside `Dog`'s override would instead call itself until the stack overflows.

