An `abstract class` is a class that's incomplete on purpose: it can declare regular, fully-implemented methods exactly like any class, but it can also declare **abstract methods** (a signature with no body at all, ending in a semicolon) that every concrete (non-abstract) subclass is *required* to implement. Java won't let you use `new` on an abstract class, even if it has a public constructor, because the object would have methods with no body to run. (A class may also be marked `abstract` without any abstract methods, purely to prevent instantiation.)

```java
public abstract class Shape {
    protected String name;

    public Shape(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public abstract double area();   // no body -- every concrete subclass must supply one

    public String describe() {       // ordinary, fully-implemented method -- inherited as-is
        return name + " has area " + area();
    }
}

public class Circle extends Shape {
    private double radius;

    public Circle(double radius) {
        super("Circle");
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override
    public double area() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

// Shape s = new Shape("generic");   // compile error: Shape is abstract; cannot be instantiated
Shape c = new Circle(3.0);          // fine -- Circle is concrete and implements area()
System.out.println(c.describe());   // 'Circle has area 28.27...'
```

Notice `describe()` calls `area()` even though `Shape` has no idea what that returns. The shared logic lives in one place, and the compiler guarantees that every concrete subclass supplies the missing piece. If `Circle` failed to override `area()`, it would itself have to be declared `abstract` too (deferring the obligation one level further down), or the code fails to compile.

An `interface` declares a set of methods a class promises to provide, with no instance state of its own (any field you declare in one is implicitly a `public static final` constant) and, traditionally, no implementation. It's a contract about *what a class can do*, separate from what it inherits from. A class uses `implements` to adopt an interface, and, critically, a class can `implement` as many interfaces as it needs, even though it can `extend` only one class.

```java
public interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}

public interface Swimmable {
    void swim();
}

public class Duck implements Flyable, Swimmable {
    @Override
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Duck flying");
    }

    @Override
    public void swim() {
        System.out.println("Duck swimming");
    }
}
```

`Duck` has no inheritance relationship at all to whatever other class might also implement `Flyable` (an `Airplane`, say); interfaces model 'can-do' capability, separate from 'is-a' ancestry. Several interfaces combine safely on one class because they bring no instance state to reconcile. Extending two classes would raise the question of which field or method body wins when they conflict (the classic 'diamond problem'), and Java avoids it by disallowing multiple class inheritance.

Since Java 8, an interface can include **default methods**: methods with a body, marked `default`, that implementing classes inherit automatically and are free to override or leave as-is. They were added so interfaces could evolve. Adding a *new* abstract method to an interface used by many existing classes would break every one of them (they'd suddenly be missing a required implementation). A default method instead ships a working fallback, so existing implementers keep compiling unchanged.

```java
public interface Greeter {
    String name();

    default String greet() {
        return "Hello, " + name() + "!";
    }
}

public class Person implements Greeter {
    private String personName;

    public Person(String personName) {
        this.personName = personName;
    }

    @Override
    public String name() {
        return personName;
    }
    // no need to implement greet() -- the default is used as-is
}

Person p = new Person("Priya");
System.out.println(p.greet());   // 'Hello, Priya!'
```

`Person` only ever implements `name()`; `greet()` comes for free from the interface's default, and it works correctly because it calls `name()`, which, at run time, dispatches to whatever implementation is providing it, like any other method call. Default methods still cannot carry instance *state*: an interface has nowhere to store per-object data, so a default method works with its parameters and the interface's other methods. (Interfaces can also have `static` methods since Java 8, and `private` helper methods since Java 9.)

